\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Spin lifts over a quadratic extension}

\p{Keep the quadratic-space hypotheses of \ref{fcap-003N}. Let
#{d\in K^\times} be nonsquare, put #{L=K(\sqrt d)}, and let #{\sigma}
be its nontrivial #{K}-automorphism. The square-class map has kernel

##{\ker j=\{1,[d]_K\}.}

Consequently, for #{g\in SO(V,Q)},

##{g_L\text{ has a Spin lift}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\nu_Q(g)\in\{1,[d]_K\}.}

The trivial class already gives a lift over #{K}. If #{\nu_Q(g)=[d]_K},
the two lifts over #{L} are exchanged by #{\sigma}; neither is the scalar
extension of a #{K}-defined Spin element.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Suppose #{a\in K^\times} becomes a square in #{L}. Write

##{a=(u+v\sqrt d)^2=u^2+dv^2+2uv\sqrt d,
\qquad u,v\in K.}

The basis #{1,\sqrt d} and characteristic different from two give
#{uv=0}. If #{v=0}, then #{a=u^2}; if #{u=0}, then #{a=dv^2}.
The nonzero remaining coefficient shows that #{[a]_K} is respectively
#{1} or #{[d]_K}. Both classes become squares in #{L}, and they are
distinct because #{d} is nonsquare. This proves the kernel formula;
\ref{fcap-003N} gives the criterion for #{g_L}.}

\p{Now assume #{\nu_Q(g)=[d]_K}. By \ref{fcap-002D}, take a
Lipschitz lift #{x} of #{g}; determinant parity makes it even.
Its reverse norm is #{db^2} for some #{b\in K^\times}.
Replacing #{x} by #{x/b} preserves its action and makes
#{N_{\mathrm{rev}}(x)=d}. In the extended Clifford algebra set

##{s=\frac{c(x)}{\sqrt d}.}

Scalar units are Lipschitz and even by \ref{fcap-002D}. Thus #{s} is
even and Lipschitz, and

##{N_{\mathrm{rev}}(s)=\frac{d}{(\sqrt d)^2}=1.}

The carrier criterion \ref{fcap-0028} places #{s} in the actual
#{\Spin(Q_L)}, and scalar normalization leaves its vector action equal
to #{g_L}. Any other Spin lift differs from #{s} by a scalar unit, by
the Lipschitz action kernel over #{L}. Norm one forces that scalar's square
to be one, so all lifts are precisely #{s} and #{-s}.}

\p{The coefficient automorphism #{\sigma} acts semilinearly on
#{\Cl(Q_L)}: it fixes each extended generator and sends #{\sqrt d}
to #{-\sqrt d}. In particular it fixes #{c(x)} and gives

##{\sigma(s)=-s,\qquad \sigma(-s)=s.}

These elements are distinct because #{s} is a unit and the characteristic
is not two. Every Clifford element obtained by extending coefficients from
#{K} is fixed by #{\sigma}. Neither lift can therefore come from
#{\Spin(Q)}, in agreement with its nontrivial spinor-norm obstruction.}
}

\p{For an explicit rational example, take #{K=\mathbb Q} and

##{Q(ae+bf)=a^2+2b^2,\qquad g=-\operatorname{Id}=r_e r_f.}

Here #{e^2=1}, #{f^2=2}, and #{ef=-fe}. Thus #{x=ef} satisfies

##{\operatorname{rev}(x)x=feef=2,\qquad x^2=efef=-2,}

and #{\nu_Q(g)=[2]_{\mathbb Q}} is nontrivial. Over
#{L=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)}, however,

##{s=\frac{ef}{\sqrt2},\qquad
N_{\mathrm{rev}}(s)=1,\qquad s^2=-1.}

Its vector action is still #{-\operatorname{Id}}. The two Spin lifts
#{s,-s} are interchanged by conjugating #{\sqrt2}; the rational orthogonal
transformation acquires lifts without either lift becoming rational.}
